  grand strategy economics economic power  is an important part of grand strategy  not necessarily so much in coercive  terms the ability to course others  economically but the ability to attract  others through your your economic  prosperity quality so I think that's an  important dimension to it that it's  about using all the tools of national  power and national policy to promote the  interests of a state not just in wartime  but but just as importantly if not more  so in in peacetime the other dimension  that I think it's important to to unpack  is that grand strategy is really about  the calculated relationship of means to  ends it's about how you take all these  various tools of national power not just  military but these other tools as well  and how you relate them to promoting and  advancing the most important interest  that state has and that's when I talk  about American grand strategy in the  Middle East that's really what I'm  trying to get at is how the United  States not just in any one  administration but across presidential  administrations not just over four year  term but over decades how the United  States goes about has gone about and  we'll go about trying to promote its  most important interests in this very  important part of the world what are the  elements of the US is grand strategy  toward the middle east I would identify  three elements that let me call them  traditional elements and then I think  there's a fourth one that's been added  on to the agenda more recently the the  three traditional elements of Mayor  eric and grand strategy in the Middle  East are first of all the provision of  energy security by protecting the  physical security of oil flows coming  out of the Persian Gulf this is  something on which the the world economy  depends and by providing this this kind  of energy security the u.s. presents  itself as doing it as a kind of  international public good it is  something that all states can benefit  from it gives the United States an  important strategic role not just in the  region but it also bolsters American  claims to global leadership and global  economic leadership even at a time when  the American economy isn't isn't going  so well and when America's international  financial position has deteriorated  quite a bit over the last decade or so  the United States still plays this very  unique role in providing this  international public good of energy  security so I think that's one important  long-standing elements American grand  strategy region a second would be trying  to encourage negotiated settlements the  various unresolved tracks of the  arab-israeli conflict American  politicians may not like to talk about  it in public very much but I think in in  policy settings in Washington there is a  very widespread recognition that there  is at least a potential for conflict  between America's interest in providing  energy security the way that I've  defined it by protecting the flow of oil  flows out of the persian gulf between  that interest and america's commitment  to a secure israel in theory at least  the peace process is the way that  successive us administrations have tried  to reconcile the any potential conflicts  between those two interests so that's a  second element a third element is that  the United States has tried over several  decades to foster a balance of power in  the region that is favorable to the  United States and its regional allies  um we typically like to divide up the  region into what we call moderate states  that are we say are interested in  stability Israel the Arab states that  have made peace with Israel other  moderate Arab states Saudi Arabian the  GCC states for example at least until  recently we would most American  politicians what I think have put turkey  in that category we have this moderate  camp on the other side we have account  we like to call radicals who are  presumably interested only in  instability the Islamic Republic of Iran  more often than not Syria various  non-state actors like Hamas Hezbollah  least until 2003 Libya might have fit in  that category and what we wanted to do  over over many years as Henry Kissinger  put it so well we want to basically  bolster the position of moderates in the  region and marginalize radicals so those  I think have been the three kind of  long-standing pillars of American grand  strategy in the region since nine eleven  we've added a fourth I think and that is  we don't want this region broadly  defined again to be a platform for  launching mass casualty terrorist  attacks against the American homeland or  other important American interests so I  think those are the basic elements of  American grand strategy in the Middle  East and what do you think is going  wrong well I think what has gone wrong  is the way in which we've we've tried to  pursue these four elements of our grand  strategy in the region the way in which  we've gone about it has grown  increasingly dysfunctional over sign I'd  identify at least a couple of different  things one is in terms of our military  posture in the region until relatively  recently we have operated in the region  as what you might  call an offshore balancer in other words  from the time that we took on this  responsibility of defending the physical  flow of oil out of the Persian Gulf we  did not at least initially we did not  try to do this by putting large numbers  of American boots on the ground we built  up a very robust naval presence in the  Gulf which we maintain we arranged all  kinds of pre positioning of military  equipment basing arrangements in various  places so that in the event of a crisis  we could surge conventional ground  forces tactical air forces into the  region but we didn't seek to deploy  those kinds of forces there in large  numbers on a permanent or ongoing basis  this is the way we fought the first Gulf  War after Saddam Hussein ordered his  armies to invade Kuwait we surged ground  forces conventional tactical air forces  into the region and with our naval  presence there use that as the basis for  expelling Iraqi forces from Kuwait I  think one of our biggest mistakes has  been we have basically gotten away from  offshore balancing as the defining  framework for a military posture in the  region we started this right after the  first Gulf War when we did not really  withdraw ground forces and tactical air  forces from the region following the end  of the Iraqi occupation of Kuwait we  kept troops on the ground and I come  back to admit why I think that was such  a disastrous decision but then we  compounded that after nine eleven with  not just the invasion of Afghanistan  which was actually something for which  we had a large amount of regional  support but we then got into a prolonged  occupation of Afghanistan which is still  basically going on and then we also  invaded Iraq and had a prolonged  occupation  of Iraq so we were no longer an offshore  balancer operating from this kind of  over the horizon military posture we  became in the eyes of most people in the  region and on the ground occupier right  now I think this had really really  profoundly negative consequences for the  United States first of all it is  directly counterproductive to our  counterterrorism goals I can draw you a  direct line from the decision to keep  forces on the ground particularly in  Saudi Arabia after the first Gulf War to  the 911 attacks bin Laden other senior  figures in al qaeda al qaeda emerged  basically in opposition to and what they  would describe as resistance to american  occupation of the Muslim holy land it is  politically convenient in popular in the  United States to say that al Qaeda  Muslim extremists attacked us on 911  because they hate our values we let  women drive we have religious toleration  all these things I mean you can just  read their websites read their  statements they don't really care what  we do in our own infidel country what  they care about is what we do in their  countries and what particularly when  they perceive is as occupying the  Arabian Peninsula the birthplace of  Islam where the two holiest places on  the planet for believing Muslims are  located that is something which is going  to draw a negative response and that  negative response culminated in the 911  attacks we then compounded that by  invading and occupying other Muslim  countries Afghanistan Iraq there are now  very very good statistical analyses  which show that the level of  anti-american terrorism has actually  increased dramatically since nine eleven  and in particular since the invasion and  occupation  of Iraq and this is directly  counterproductive to what we say our  counterterrorism goals in the region I  also think in the end invasion and  occupation in the Middle East it just  doesn't work very well you know it  underscores the limits of American  military power which diminishes our  strategic standing in the region and I  think it also really for many regional  constituencies whether we like it or not  it fundamentally calls into question the  legitimacy of our purposes in the region  very few people in the region believe we  are actually doing good by prolonged  occupation of Afghanistan by prolonged  occupation of Iraq and we are really  undermining the legitimacy of American  engagement in the region by by doing  that finally to say even for countries  outside the region like China it really  has called into question whether we  really are providing a public good  saying the form of energy security from  a Chinese perspective the way the United  States has acted militarily in the  region over the last several years it's  actually more of a threat to Chinese  energy security that it is a help at  least in their in their perception and I  think that to create some real damage  for our international position the  second thing that we've done that I  think is really really hurt us is we  basically have tanked on providing any  meaningful leadership on arab-israeli  diplomacy there are a couple of  different dimensions to this one just in  terms of how we act as a mediator we  love to say that we can't want peace  more than the parties but the factor  matter is we can in fact well peace more  than the parties and the reality is we  almost certainly do want peace more than  the parties but  we have let ourselves be put in a  position where we basically don't want  to do anything in the peace process  which will make any particular sitting  Israeli government uncomfortable so  we've effectively tanked on providing  meaningful leadership in that in that  process beyond that we have essentially  bought an Israeli argument that Israeli  security requires something that's  tantamount to Israeli military hegemony  in the region that israel needs to be  able to use force first or at least  unilaterally anytime anywhere for more  or less any purpose that it deems  desirable and that has really i think  undermined American credibility in the  region and done real damage to our  standing and influence we are no longer  seen as strategically dominant power in  the region that's actually trying to  solve problems for people in the region  we are seen as basically a power with  hegemonic aspirations which is creating  instability furthering conflict and  backing Israel in what is widely seen as  its hegemonic ambitions in the region I  think that has done real damage as well  so how do we get back on the road to  recovery so to speak um what do we need  to do several things one is I think it's  really important that we return to this  offshore balance or roll with an over  the horizon military posture you know  the troops that we have on the ground in  Iraq in Afghanistan they are not serving  a positive long-term strategic purpose  for the United States and we should be  moving back as rapidly as possible to  this over the horizon posture that's one  thing secondly I would really avoid  another what I would call a war of  choice in  Middle East if we are going to have  another war in the Middle East it will  be a war with Iran it will be a war that  the United States initiates and it will  be a war that the United States  initiates essentially because the  Islamic Republic is enriching uranium  there's not going to be a smoking gun  where we have intelligence that they're  actually trying to fabricate nuclear  weapons the International Atomic Energy  Agency is not going to differ is not  going to detect diversion of nuclear  materials from Iranian nuclear  facilities we're not going to have that  kind of causes belly if we go to war  it's going to be because we reach a  point in consultation with the Israelis  that we don't want the Iranians to go  any further in their own uranium  enrichment efforts it will have no  international legitimation there will be  no security council resolution that can  be even remotely construed as  authorizing it the sanctions resolutions  in place on Iran the Chinese and the  Russians have insisted that language be  put in there which makes it clear there  is no legal basis in these resolutions  for the United States to use force if we  do it we are going to be seen as a kind  of rogue superpower unconstrained by  international law international  institutions and it is going to have an  enormously negative imperative already  seen that way though um place it's going  to it's going to take it to a new level  that thing and I think it will also  raise further questions for countries  like China keep in mind if we fight  another war in the Middle East we're to  be fighting this one on barham money and  it's going to be money borrowed from  China from other countries that are not  going to be very thrilled to see a  military another military confrontation  in this critical part of the world are  they really going to be willing you know  to finance something that is from their  perspective so negative for their own  interests I think it's going to end up  creating real  for us internationally and the potential  for reveal it's going to be our own kind  of Suez moment and illusion in the  nineteen fifty-six who has crisis where  Britain overstepped and basically set  itself up for the end of any perception  of the United Kingdom as a real major  player in international affairs I think  it would do real profoundly negative  damage to America's international  position so avoid that I'd also say in  terms of the balance of power in the  region now it is critical that the  United States realign its relations with  the Islamic Republic of Iran as  thoroughly as comprehensively as we  realign our relations with the People's  Republic of China in the early 1970s at  this point in the evolution the regional  balance of power because of a lot of  different factors including American  mistakes we are in a position where we  now cannot achieve any of our own  high-priority objectives in this part of  the world in arab-israeli stuff in  providing energy security in Iraq and  Afghanistan we can't achieve any of our  own most important objectives without a  better more productive relationship with  Iran so I think that's critical to do I  think it's very important that we adopt  a genuinely regional strategy on  Afghanistan we are going to have to have  all the players in Afghanistan at the  table to come up with a legitimate  sustainable power-sharing arrangement in  that country and the only way we're  going to get there is if we have various  key regional players including Iran  helping us to organize that kind of  table now that's going to mean at what I  would call a genuinely regional strategy  I think we're also related to the idea  that will go back to offshore balancing  we have to get back to the idea that we  really are a strategically dominant  power which is providing real public  goods for people in the region for  people outside of the region you know  strategic primacy these  days is something which is very hard to  assert unilaterally it's something which  other players have to legitimate and I  think we need to get back on a track  through these various steps which will  help us rebuild some measure of  legitimacy for leading American role in  this part of the world in light of these  steps are things you just outlined what  do you think the best case scenario is  and the worst case scenario is in terms  of grand strategy in the Middle East the  best case scenario would be well if I  think if my recommendations were  followed I think you would see the  United States regain a good deal of its  strategic standing and influence in the  region it would be seen once again as a  as a power that was pursuing purposes  which a lot of regional constituencies  would be prepared to see as legitimate a  lot of international constituencies  would be prepared to see is legitimate  and we would restore a greater measure  of influence in this important part of  the region I think taking a very  different approach to the arab-israeli  arenas also essential to that that we  basically need a different kind of peace  process than we've got we have a kind of  Cinderella shoe peace process where if  you meet certain criteria that we define  we might let you come to the table but  it means we don't have the right people  at the table to reach a deal and if the  people at the table could reach any kind  of deal it wouldn't be sustainable it  wouldn't be implementable it certainly  wouldn't be legitimate so I think that's  another part of this really gitam a shin  of American power and influence in the  region the worst case scenario is we  basically stay on the track that we're  on and I think what when I say we're  headed toward strategic failure it means  that we're headed we are increasingly  um less and less effective at achieving  our own goals in this region the United  States is significantly less capable of  achieving its stated policy objectives  in the Middle East and the Persian Gulf  today than it was ten years ago you  extrapolate that out another five years  another ten years we may still have this  unique capacity to project a lot of  military power in the region but we are  going to be seen basically as a failed  hegemon a country which cannot achieve  its own purposes in the region and  cannot marshal other countries to work  with it to make they have these other  countries see our objectives as in some  way consistent with or supportive of  their own objectives and I think that's  going to be if that hat plays out that's  going to be a very very damaging blow to  America's global position what do you  think the reality of what actually will  happen Jake I'm sorry to say i'm not  particularly optimistic as an American I  hope I'm wrong in saying that but as an  analyst as a scholar I have to say I'm  not very optimistic a lot of reasons for  that the way our own domestic politics  is works as part of it I think coming  out of the Cold War americans believe  that they were on top of the world they  didn't really have to make strategic  choices they didn't really have to make  hard or difficult strategic calculations  of the sort you have to make to have a  really viable grand strategy and I think  that they are in some ways not just  politically we're not just politically  constrained but we're culturally not  very inclined to do it and basically out  of practice at doing it whether we can  recover get over those constraints yet  beyond those cultural  predilections and and and get back into  the business of doing real grand  strategy in time to avoid strategic  failure is an open question but I have  to say I'm not not overly optimistic  about it well very good thank you so  much for being here with us today and  sharing some of your work thank you for  more information about mr. Leverett and  his work please visit our website at  yale edu backslash macmillan report be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you

